    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d120">

                <pb facs="0385" n="352" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v13/pg352"/>

                <head>120. Transcript of a Telephone Conversation Between Secretary of Defense
                        Laird and the President’s Assistant
                    for National Security Affairs (Kissinger)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d120-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1971-02-20T18:13:00-05:00">February 20, 1971,
                            6:13 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion of the military situation in Laos.]</p>
                <p>L: One other point on the Baltic. I understood you were going to discuss that
                        yesterday<anchor n="2" xml:id="d120-f2"/> and you didn’t get around to
                    it.</p>
                <p>K: In the afternoon. That’s right.</p>
                <p>L: When will you do that? We told them they would be able to put forth their
                    points.</p>
                <p>K: If they have complaints let them come to me. The President wants it.</p>
                <p>L: I would like to make as many port calls in the Baltic as we had in 69.</p>
                <pb facs="0386" n="353" xml:id="pg353"/>
                <p>K: We want something cruising around in the next few weeks to get the message
                    [across].</p>
                <p>L: Do you think they will?</p>
                <p>K: They will.</p>
                <p>L: Did the meeting work out okay?</p>
                <p>K: It was fine and it was [omission in transcript]. From now he will assume
                    [when] you have a complaint you will make it and not listen to anyone.</p>
                <p>L: Pursley spoke to Haig.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d120-f3"/> Gardner
                    said they wanted him to make suggestions on improvements. I said I hope you
                    didn’t give the impression we were upset.</p>
                <p>K: We have no complaints. It went well.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d120-f4"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d120-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d120-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon
                    Presidential Materials, Henry Kissinger
                    Telephone Conversation Transcripts, Box 8, Chronological File. No classification
                    marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d120-f2" n="2" xml:id="d120-f2-content">
                <p>Reference is presumably to the WSAG meeting on
                    February 18. See <ref target="#d117">Document 117</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d120-f3" n="3" xml:id="d120-f3-content">
                <p>On the morning of February 19, DIA reported that Navy patrol aircraft had spotted
                    a Soviet guided-missile light cruiser 210 nautical miles northwest of Havana:
                    “Call Haig on Baltic/Black.” “Pls
                    advise if you have other info.” (Washington National Records Center, RG 330, OSD Files:
                        FRC 330–76–197, Box 63, Cuba 323.3)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d120-f4" n="4" xml:id="d120-f4-content">
                <p>On February 24, Laird formally approved a
                    plan, outlined by Moorer on February 12,
                    to deploy 4 destroyers in the Black Sea for 11 days, starting on March 27.
                    (Memorandum from Laird to Moorer, February 24; ibid., FRC 330–74–115, Box 3, 560)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
